-- title:
--   Coset factorisation of the modular equation over K((q))
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, $M$ a nonzero natural number, and `data` a [`ModularCurve.ModularPolynomialData M`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L215), that is a polynomial $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ that is monic in $Y$, of $Y$-degree $\psi(M) = \sum_{d \mid M,\ d \text{ squarefree}} M/d$, and satisfies $\Phi(j(q), j(q^M)) = 0$ in the rational Laurent series. Let $\zeta \in K^\times$ be a primitive $M$-th root of unity. Write $J =$ `jqModC K` for the Laurent series $q^{-1}$ times the image in $K$ of the integral power series $E_4^3 \cdot \eta$-unit-inverse (the $q$-expansion of $j$ over $K$), let `jqNField K M` be the intermediate field $K(\,$`qExpand K M` $J\,)$ of `LaurentSeries K` generated by the $M$-th substituted expansion, and let `data.toJqNField K` be $\Phi$ with $X$ evaluated at that generator, a polynomial over `jqNField K M`. The conclusion is fourfold: (i) the image of this polynomial in `LaurentSeries K` equals $\prod_t (Y -$`cosetConj` $\zeta\, J\, t)$, the product over the triples $t = (a,b,d)$ with $ad = M$, $b < d$ and $\gcd(a,\gcd(b,d)) = 1$ (the finite set `primCosetReps M`), where `cosetConj` $\zeta\, J\,(a,b,d)$ is $J$ with $q$ replaced by $\zeta^{ab} q^{a^2}$ (and $0$ when $a = 0$); (ii) that image splits into linear factors over `LaurentSeries K`; (iii) `data.toJqNField K` is separable over `jqNField K M`; (iv) its root set in `LaurentSeries K` is exactly the image of `primCosetReps M` under $t \mapsto$ `cosetConj` $\zeta\, J\, t$.
--
--   This is the coset factorisation of the modular equation $\Phi_M(j(q^M), Y) = \prod_{ad = M,\, 0 \le b < d,\, \gcd(a,b,d)=1} (Y - j(\zeta^{ab}q^{a^2}))$, here over an arbitrary field containing a primitive $M$-th root of unity rather than only over $\mathbb{C}$. It supplies the root-set and separability input for the identification of roots of the modular polynomial in the construction of $X_0(M)$, and is used by the results that recover $j(q^M)$-type expansions from vanishing of $\Phi_M$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_ModularPolynomialData_map_adjoin_jqNModC_eq_cosetTwoVarPoly.lean

